{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>You shall not take the name of HaShem your Elohim [in vain].</b> שֵׁם is like זֵכֶר, and it is drawn to be a sign for the one named. But זָכַר is found in the sacred tongue sometimes in the heart and sometimes in the mouth, whereas the שֵׁם is borne on the mouth. So too we find that David said, “and I will not take their names upon my lips” (Psalms 16:4). Now the reason for remembering the Name: since the Name is truth, so too His word is truth. If one does not fulfill his word, it is as though he denies the Name. The practice of the people of Egypt to this day: if a man swears by the life of the king and does not fulfill his word, he is liable to death, and even if he gave a ransom of his weight in gold he would not live, because he has dishonored the king in public. If so, for a king of flesh and blood — how many thousands upon thousands of times is a man obligated to guard himself lest his tongue cause him to stumble, to let his mouth sin against his flesh, by bearing [the Name] for vanity.\n\nNow we have seen that because the Israelites swore a public oath concerning the concubine at Gibeah, and Pinchas the priest was there, we found that those who transgressed the oath were killed, and likewise the men of Jabesh-gilead, men, women, and children. And so too this is not done even with Sabbath desecrators. We also saw that Saul sought to kill Jonathan his son — and he had not heard the oath. We saw that HaShem brought famine upon the land because of Saul and his house, who violated the oath of the princes who swore to the Gibeonites (Joshua 9:15); and when those of the blood-house were killed, then “HaShem responded to the land” (2 Samuel 21:14). And Solomon said to Shimei, “Did I not adjure you by HaShem?” (1 Kings 2:42).\n\nThe principle: we do not find in the Ten Commandments an explicitly stated reward written except for honoring father and mother, nor an explicitly stated punishment except for idolatry and taking the Name in vain. Many think that one who takes the Name in vain has not committed a great transgression. I will show them that it is more severe than all the prohibitions that come after it: for the murderer and the adulterer, which are grave transgressions, cannot at all times murder or commit adultery, for he will be afraid. But one who has accustomed himself to swearing in vain will swear in a single day oaths without number. He is so habituated to this transgression that he does not know he swore, and if you rebuke him, “Why did you swear just now?” — then he will swear that he did not swear, out of the force of his habit in it. For before every utterance they speak, they preface it with an oath, and it is for them a figure of speech. If there were in Israel only this transgression alone, it would suffice to prolong the exile and to add blow upon blow to our blows.\n\nI will show their folly: the murderer, if he killed his enemy, his desire for vengeance was fulfilled; the adulterer too, for his moment; the thief found benefit for his needs; the false witness, to ingratiate himself or to take vengeance. But one who swears for falsehood at all times when no oath is upon him profanes the Name of Heaven in public for no benefit that he has."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The lemma lacks the concluding phrase לשוא ('in vain') present in the biblical text; the omission appears to be Ibn Ezra's trimming of the lemma and is preserved as-is."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:22.398417781Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}